Assessment of vehicle dynamics using highperformance driving simulator
Zhou Fang, Jean-Michel Nidiot, Jean-Christophe Collinet, Dominique Theillier, Florent Colombet, Didier Wautier, Stéphane Régnier
Renault TCR, France, {zhou.fang, jean-michel.nidiot, jean-christophe.collinet, dominique.theillier, florent.colombet, didier.wautier, stephane.regnier}@renault.com

Abstract – The aim of this work is to establish a practical evaluation method to measure the performances of driving simulators and the motion cueing algorithms in terms of relative or absolute fidelity. To achieve this objective, we investigate two driving experiences for lateral vehicle dynamics using a high-performance driving simulator. One scenario involves normal freeway driving tests with slalom maneuvers, while the other entails driving at the tire-adherent limit condition on a handling track. We compare the performance of motion cueing algorithms based on classical filter (CF-MCA) and model predictive control (MPC-MCA) using the method developed in this study which is consistent with the ratings given by professional drivers. Thanks to this work, a vehicle handling assessment on high lateral dynamics has been achieved on ROADS (Renault Operational Advanced Driving Simulator) and is applicable for all vehicles’ project development.

Keywords: MCA performance, driving simulation for vehicle dynamics assessment, tilt velocity threshold.

1. Introduction
Driving simulators have a wide range of applications: driver training, optimizing race car design, such as the F1 driving simulators at Renault Sport and McLaren in the UK, road infrastructure design (e.g., Tongji University driving simulator), vehicle development with the driver in the loop, testing critical situations without exposing drivers to real danger, etc. In recent years, with the growing development of AD/ADAS and electric vehicles, some car manufacturers have developed highperformance driving simulators (e.g., Daimler, Nissan, BMW, Renault, etc.). The main aim of these high-performance driving simulators is to reduce the gap in motion perception between the driving simulator and the real car, thereby improving the fidelity of the driving simulation and making the subjective evaluation results more representative of the physical tests. Thus, by using an advanced driving simulator, we can carry out subjective evaluations using digital models upstream of the project. As described in the literature (Kharrazi et al., 2019), driving simulators can be used for iterative testing at an early stage to better gather subjective understanding of various vehicle designs. This will enable vehicle designs to be evaluated and refined before moving on to real-world testing, thereby reducing development time and improving design quality.

In the literature, we found a few articles dealing with the subjective assessment of vehicle dynamics using a driving simulator (Fischer et al., 2010; Berthoz et al., 2013; Kharrazi et al., 2019; Lamprecht et al., 2022), but these were generally limited to a level of lateral acceleration on freeways between 2 and 3 m/s². Studies investigating high lateral dynamics in a motion-based driving simulator (Fang et al., 2020) are still rare. To fill this gap, the present study investigates the subjective assessment of high lateral dynamics based on different scenarios ranging from highway driving experiences with slalom to handling tracks. The aim of this work is to establish a practical evaluation method in terms of relative or absolute fidelity for Renault’s high performance driving simulator ROADS.

