3. Potential workspace of a driving simulator
The potential workspace or more precisely the potential reproduction area of acceleration in a driving simulator is defined by the acceleration level and its maximal duration that can be simulated. We

will establish this potential operating area considering human motion perception thresholds. We refer to this potential operating zone as a "-T chart" for simplicity.
In a dark room, the perceived linear motion threshold is 0.05m/s² for sinusoidal excitation (Benson et al., 1986). In a driving simulator with high dynamic scenarios, this threshold could increase to 0.1m/s², or even more (cf. table 2). The perceived tilt velocity in a driving simulator generally ranges from 3°/s to 6°/s, depending on the current acceleration amplitude reproduced by rail and hexapod. Our experience in a high dynamic scenario with high rail motion shows that a tilt velocity of 4°/s, or even 56°/s in some instances of high peak acceleration, can be accepted by most people. Therefore, a linear acceleration of 0.1m/s² and a tilt velocity of 4°/s are used to construct the  -T map.
