To reproduce constant acceleration motion, the cockpit is generally accelerated constantly for a duration of T seconds, followed by a long braking phase to stop at the end of the maximal rail position. The braking phase is controlled with a deceleration level below the human perception threshold. Figure 3 illustrates an example of ROADS X or Y rail performance for reproducing 1g acceleration using ubrake = 0.1m/s². The reproduced period (=0.18s) is very short for this example. By employing an opposite motion strategy, named "back-go strategy" to generate negative velocity (Fang, 2010, see Fig. 4), the simulated duration of acceleration can be doubled. However, the second case is not applicable for an active driving test, except for some eventtriggered tests where the car’s trajectory is always the same and the vehicle speed is repeated in each test. To increase the simulated acceleration duration, we can also use tilt coordination technique to achieve higher braking motion without being perceived by the driver, thus significantly extending the reproduced duration.
Figure 4 illustrates the principle of the strategy using tilt-coordination and pre-velocity technique. The principle of the “back-go strategy” with tilt is mainly used in MPC-MCA or optimal-MCA (Venrooij et al., 2016).

