As depicted in Fig. 5, the lowpass scaling factor should ideally be around 0.3-0.4 for the CTA1 scenario. It's important to note that a real-time filter with minimal data buffering latency introduces some phase distortion to the filtered signal. Specifically, the phase of high-frequency signals is advanced while low-frequency signals present a delay (Fang et al., 2020). If the lowpass and highpass scaling factors have the same value, the resultant reference signal's phase aligns perfectly with the original signal. However, in some use-cases, drivers prefer more feedback from high-frequency signals to better understand the vehicle's behavior. Unfortunately, when using two different scaling factors, the phase of the resultant signal is distorted compared to the original. These scaling factors need to be tuned based on the performance of the motion cueing algorithm and the driver's tolerance for false cues and phase distortion.
4. MCA description
To evaluate MCA performance, we compare two motion cueing algorithms: one based on classical filter technique, a widely used MCA, and another, our internal Model Predictive Control based MCA (MPCMCA).
To ensure consistent motion perception across lateral acceleration, roll, and yaw velocity motions, all three signals are scaled using the same lowfrequency scaling factor, KLP, and high-frequency scaling factor, KHP. The cutoff frequency of the applied filter is also the same for these three motions.

