6. Results
6.1 MCAs performance
To facilitate the MCAs’ performance comparison, in this paper, the MPC-MCA data comes from a driving experience recording and the result of CF-MCA is simulated using the same input signal as the MPCMCA.
Fig. 9 directly compares the motion cueing results between CF-MCA and MPC-MCA for the
N104 scenario. The specific force provided by MPCMCA closely matches the vehicle’s lateral acceleration on a 1:1 scale, while CF-MCA provides a motion cueing result scaled at 0.8:1 with a low linear correlation coefficient (R²=0.81, see table 10). Both MCAs ensure tilt velocities below 3°/s.
Figs. 10-11 compare the motion cueing results of MPC-MCA and CF-MCA for the CTA1 scenario. A substantial difference is observed in the amplitude of the reproduced lowpass motion signal between CFMCA and MPC-MCA.
Fig. 12 depicts the roll velocity of the MPC-MCA as well as the tilt velocity (deviation from the scaled roll velocity of the vehicle).

To produce more tilt specific force during moments of high lateral acceleration, the tilt velocity exceeds the restricted nominal value of 4°/s in MCA. While professional drivers have tested the scenario and not reported issues, future iterations of MCA should aim to reduce these high tilt velocities.
Figs. 13-18 present classification results of motion cues for both MCAs.
For the N104 scenario (Figs. 13-14), significant differences in the indicators of false cues between CF-MCA and MPC-MCA are observed for signals of all frequency bands. Note that high frequency motion cues (fc HP = 0.45Hz) are effectively reproduced by both MCAs with scaling factors exceeding 0.95 and R²>0.95 (see tables 9-10).
For the CTA1 scenario (Figs. 15-18), MPC-MCA achieves an effective scaling factor three times higher than CF-MCA for low-frequency signals (Figs 15-16), but with higher index of false cues due to increased motion stimuli reproduction. Feedback from drivers underscores the importance of lowfrequency motion amplitude in driving experience. Similarly, the effective scaling factor of highfrequency signals for MPC-MCA surpasses that of CF-MCA with 35% more gain (see Figs 17-18). Both MCAs achieve >90% correctness of motion cues.
